  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Herman Westerink\u2019s <em>A Dark Trace</em> shows clear scholarly and educational reach: it has 10 OpenAlex citations, 22,750 recorded downloads through OAPEN, and evidence of use across psychology, psychoanalysis, theology, philosophy, and related fields. The book is also openly accessible, which likely supports its uptake, and its thematic profile aligns with questions of gender equality and reduced inequalities.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\"><em>A Dark Trace</em> is a 2021 scholarly book from Leuven University Press that follows Freud\u2019s reflections on guilt, tracing how that concept moves between individual psychic life, childhood, and the wider prehistory of culture and religion. The record shows a work with visible academic traction and sustained use: OpenAlex reports 10 citations, and the book has been downloaded 22,750 times via OAPEN. Its percentile standing indicates a strong position within its field and year context, and the open-access version likely supports this pattern of reach.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The citation record suggests that the book\u2019s influence is interdisciplinary rather than confined to one niche. Citing works appear in psychoanalysis, psychiatry, psychology, theology, religious studies, and even adjacent humanities and social-science contexts such as marketing and queer studies. That pattern fits the book\u2019s core themes, especially its engagement with guilt, moral judgment, religion, and the psychoanalytic tradition. The citing landscape is modest in volume but broad in disciplinary spread, which is often a sign of conceptual rather than purely technical uptake.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Usage evidence is especially strong. The download count shows substantial reader interest over time, including repeated monthly activity in the OAPEN record. This is an important complement to citations: it indicates that the book is being consulted and read, not only referenced in later publications. The book is also recorded as open access in the repository environment, which broadens availability for researchers, students, and independent readers.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Thematic and social relevance signals also point to wider significance. The digest links the book to Sustainable Development Goals on reduced inequalities and gender equality, reflecting the social and ethical questions embedded in its treatment of guilt, normativity, and culture. Teaching-related evidence is limited in the record provided, but the WorldCat holding signal suggests library discoverability and potential classroom accessibility. Overall, the evidence supports a picture of a book with sustained scholarly use, strong open-access reach, and interdisciplinary relevance.</p>",
